00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:05.010
Chris: Software maintenance is a key part
of the software development life cycle.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:11.030
Its purpose is to modify and continuously
update software applications to eliminate all

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:17.099
possible errors, malfunctions, to improve work
efficiency and better system performance and

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.199
ensure the software meets customer’s needs.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:24.150
Leah: Software developers don’t have the luxury
of launching a product and then forgetting

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:28.730
about it, they constantly need to be on the
lookout to both correct and improve their

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:31.230
software to remain competitive and relevant.

00:00:31.799 --> 00:00:36.479
Developing a piece of software can take a
couple of years but maintenance is ongoing

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.070
for the lifetime of the product and can
last up to 20 years and sometimes even more.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.180
Let’s look at the four main
types of software maintenance.

00:00:46.099 --> 00:00:47.320
Chris: Corrective maintenance

00:00:47.969 --> 00:00:52.930
‘Corrective software maintenance’ is the typical,
classic form of maintenance, if something goes

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:55.280
wrong and then a solution is developed to fix it.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.460
Corrective software maintenance is necessary
when a piece of software shows faults and

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:06.990
errors, or perhaps there is a compatibility issue
with another piece of newly released software.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:11.679
These can have a widespread impact
on functionality and therefore must

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.550
be addressed as quickly as possible.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.630
Leah: Frequently software developers can address
issues that require ‘corrective maintenance’

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.600
due to error or bug reports that users send in.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:28.990
If a company can recognize and take care
of faults before users discover them, then

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:33.810
this is an added advantage that will make
a company seem more reputable and reliable

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.680
Chris: Adaptive maintenance

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:41.669
Technology is constantly changing, and it is
of critical importance that software is able

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:46.919
to keep up with these changes and ‘Adaptive
maintenance’ takes this into consideration.

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:51.970
There may be a new version of an operating
system launched or a new type of processor

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.460
that will affect how the software performs.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.320
Sometimes, adaptive software maintenance reflects
organizational policies or rules as well.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:06.650
Updating services, making modifications to
vendors, or changing payment processors can

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:09.230
all necessitate adaptive software maintenance.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:16.579
Leah: A typical example is the infamous Y2K
bug of 1999, where companies believed their

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:25.150
software would no longer function as the
date shifted from 31/12/1999 to 1/1/2000.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:31.260
In response, developers worked to prepare
their software in anticipation of Y2K problems.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:35.589
If they had failed to do so, they
would be dealing with a corrective

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:38.019
maintenance problem on January 1.

00:02:38.799 --> 00:02:40.099
Chris: Perfective maintenance

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.070
‘Perfective software maintenance’
focuses on features that improve the user

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:47.340
experience through functional enhancements.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.760
It's about improving the system performance
in ways that aren't specifically in

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.460
response to a fault or problem, but
instead in response to customer feedback.

00:02:57.059 --> 00:03:04.060
Examples are ‘code functionality enhancements’,
‘usability changes’, or ‘speed optimization’,

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:08.890
all of which improve the value of
software and enhance the user experience.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.549
Leah: Preventive Software Maintenance

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:16.440
‘Preventive Software Maintenance’ is
about software changes and adaptations.

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:21.930
These reduce the risk of deterioration,
as software is operated for a long time.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.980
Preventative Software Maintenance
improves maintainability and

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:31.209
stability, through code optimization,
restructuring, and documentation updates.

00:03:31.969 --> 00:03:36.400
Examples may include new features to
improve reliability and performance, and

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:42.120
refining software to reduce complexity to
make it easier to maintain and understand.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:47.739
Chris: As we all know software systems age
and ‘preventive software maintenance’ is about

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:54.060
future-proofing its longevity and efficacy by
implementing good practices for long-term gain.

